Wasserstein Distortion (WD) is a perceptual image-to-image metric that compares spatially local estimates of statistics (mean and standard deviation) in deep feature space, allowing textures with large pointwise differences but similar local statistics to be considered perceptually similar. Unlike pointwise metrics such as LPIPS Computation which penalize phase shifts and exact pixel alignment, WD models human peripheral vision by aggregating features over local pooling regions, making it suitable for optimizing perceptual texture quality in differentiable rendering. Inputs and Outputs: Inputs: Ground truth image I{	ext{gt}} and reconstructed image I{	ext{pred}}. Outputs: A scalar distortion value D{	ext{WD}}. Data Structures: Feature maps F{	ext{gt}}, F{	ext{pred}}: Multi-scale representations extracted from a pre-trained VGG network. Pooling Kernel: A spatial window centered at each pixel used to compute local statistics. Parameters: \sigma: Hyperparameter controlling the trade-off between point-wise fidelity (\sigma 	o 0) and texture re-sampling/peripheral vision mimicry (larger \sigma). Typical Value: \sigma = 4 (constant). Spatially Varying \sigma(x,y): Optional, allowing greater flexibility by allocating more weight to salient regions. Kernel Size: Sources disagree on the exact definition; one defines the pooling kernel size directly as \sigma, while the other defines it as 2\sigma. Steps: 1. Feature Extraction: Extract multi-scale feature representations F{	ext{gt}} and F{	ext{pred}} from the pre-trained VGG network for the ground truth and reconstructed images respectively. 2. Local Statistics Computation: For each feature map and each spatial location (x, y), apply a pooling kernel centered at (x, y) to the feature map. Compute the local mean \mu and local standard deviation 
u of the features within this kernel. Let \mu, 
